WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.500
A video is a wonderful way to explain an idea. But when you need that idea again, a timeline is a surprisingly difficult place to search.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Start with the source. Preserve the title and a link to the original. A useful note should always tell you where its information came from.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Next, keep the timestamps. A timestamp is more than a label: it is a way back to the speaker, the demonstration, and the context around a claim.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Use existing subtitles when they are available. That avoids downloading an entire video or running speech recognition for words that already exist.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:03.000
When there are no subtitles, transcribe the audio locally. Once the model has been downloaded, your recordings can stay on your own machine.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Finally, choose a format that travels. Markdown works in a text editor, a knowledge base, version control, and the tools you use to think with AI.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:32.000
A summary can help you decide what to read. Keep the full transcript alongside it so you can verify a statement rather than relying on the summary alone.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:45.000
The goal is simple: spend less time looking for what you watched, and more time doing something useful with what you learned.
